{% comment %}
  sections/overlays.liquid

  Everything that floats above the page and is not the cart or search: the toast,
  the quick option drawer, the image zoom modal and the compare drawer.

  They live in a section so a merchant can turn individual ones off, and so the
  Theme Editor can re-render them without touching the layout. Each is gated on
  the setting that makes it reachable, so nothing ships markup for a feature that
  is switched off.
{% endcomment %}

{% render 'toast-notification' %}

{%- comment -%}
  Quick add is a modal, not a drawer.

  It used to be `<quick-option-drawer class="quick-view drawer" data-placement="end">`
  and it is neither of those things. A drawer is somewhere a customer goes and
  comes back from — the cart, the menu, the filters — and sliding the page aside
  to ask which flavour they want turns one inline choice into a navigation. Quick
  add interrupts the page the customer is reading and hands it straight back, so
  it takes the centre of the screen and the modal's own entrance.

  The markup is `<modal-dialog>`'s, class for class: `.modal`, `.modal__dialog`,
  `.modal__panel`. Nothing here is a quick-add-shaped copy of a modal — the
  overlay behaviour, the backdrop, the entrance and the close button placement
  are the ones `assets/base.css` already gives every modal in the theme, and
  `.quick-add` adds only the width and the body's scroll.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if settings.enable_quick_add -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    ---- the frosted backdrop, and the panel on it ----

    A `{% style %}` block rather than custom properties on the element, and that
    is not a preference: `::backdrop` is the one thing on the page that cannot
    be reached by inheriting a value onto an ancestor. It has no parent element
    in the original spec — browsers only began inheriting it from the
    originating element in 2023, and relying on that would mean the blur worked
    in a recent Chrome and silently did nothing in a Safari a year older.

    A rule that targets the pseudo-element directly works everywhere, and both
    halves of the design read from the same two numbers, so the page behind and
    the panel over it are frosted to the same degree.

    Opacity goes into the colour rather than onto the element: `opacity` on the
    panel would fade the product inside it along with its background.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign qa_blur = settings.quick_add_blur | default: 20 | plus: 0

    assign qa_backdrop_alpha = settings.quick_add_backdrop_opacity | default: 20 | divided_by: 100.0
    assign qa_backdrop = settings.quick_add_backdrop_color | default: '#ffffff'
    assign qa_backdrop = qa_backdrop | color_modify: 'alpha', qa_backdrop_alpha

    assign qa_panel_alpha = settings.quick_add_panel_opacity | default: 85 | divided_by: 100.0
    assign qa_panel = settings.quick_add_panel_color | default: '#ffffff'
    assign qa_panel = qa_panel | color_modify: 'alpha', qa_panel_alpha
  -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The backdrop's own entrance.

    It had none. The panel animated and the backdrop was simply *there* on the
    frame the dialog opened and gone on the frame it closed — a hard cut behind
    a moving panel, which reads as the jump it is. The two now run to the same
    numbers, so the page dims as the modal arrives and lifts as it leaves.

    It has to be an animation rather than a transition, and for a reason that
    is specific to `::backdrop`: the pseudo-element does not exist until
    `showModal()` creates it, and a transition needs a previous value to move
    from. An animation has its own first frame.

    The state comes from `data-state` on the overlay element, which
    `assets/dialog.js` writes for exactly this — `opening`, `open`, `closing`.
    Both open states name the same animation, so the attribute changing from
    one to the other does not restart it.

    The exit is three quarters of the entrance, which is what `exitDuration` in
    `assets/dialog.js` uses, so the backdrop and the panel finish together
    rather than one waiting on the other.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign qa_enter = settings.quick_add_animation_duration | default: 320 | plus: 0
    assign qa_exit = qa_enter | times: 0.75 | round
  -%}

  {% style %}
    @keyframes quick-add-backdrop-in {
      from { opacity: 0; }
      to { opacity: 1; }
    }

    @keyframes quick-add-backdrop-out {
      from { opacity: 1; }
      to { opacity: 0; }
    }

    #QuickAdd .modal__dialog::backdrop {
      background-color: {{ qa_backdrop }};
      {%- if qa_blur > 0 %}
        -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur({{ qa_blur }}px);
        backdrop-filter: blur({{ qa_blur }}px);
      {%- endif %}
    }

    #QuickAdd[data-state='opening'] .modal__dialog::backdrop,
    #QuickAdd[data-state='open'] .modal__dialog::backdrop {
      animation: quick-add-backdrop-in {{ qa_enter }}ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1) both;
    }

    #QuickAdd[data-state='closing'] .modal__dialog::backdrop {
      animation: quick-add-backdrop-out {{ qa_exit }}ms cubic-bezier(0.76, 0, 0.24, 1) both;
    }

    {%- comment -%}
      The dimming stays, the movement goes. A backdrop that faded out under
      reduced motion would still be a backdrop appearing instantly on open,
      which is the thing being fixed — so both ends simply land at once.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      #QuickAdd[data-state] .modal__dialog::backdrop {
        animation-duration: 0.01ms;
      }
    }

    #QuickAdd .modal__panel {
      background-color: {{ qa_panel }};
      {%- comment -%}
        Only when the panel is actually see-through. A blur behind an opaque
        surface is a compositing layer the browser maintains for every frame
        the modal is open and nobody can see the result of.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if qa_blur > 0 and qa_panel_alpha < 1 %}
        -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur({{ qa_blur }}px);
        backdrop-filter: blur({{ qa_blur }}px);
      {%- endif %}
    }
  {% endstyle %}

  {%- comment -%}
    The entrance and its length are the merchant's — **Theme settings › Product
    cards › Quick add modal**. They are attributes rather than a stylesheet
    variable because the movement is a WAAPI animation on the panel, built from
    keyframes in `assets/dialog.js`: a modal that is closed has no box to
    transition, so the entrance cannot be a CSS transition and the panel's
    resting transform has to be left alone between openings.

    `<modal-dialog>` clamps the duration and falls back to its own default for a
    name it does not know, so a stale saved value can never leave a modal that
    takes three seconds to appear or one that never arrives.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <quick-add-modal
    id="QuickAdd"
    class="quick-add modal"
    data-overlay-type="quick-add"
    data-animation="{{ settings.quick_add_animation | default: 'zoom-in' }}"
    data-animation-duration="{{ settings.quick_add_animation_duration | default: 320 }}"
  >
    <dialog class="modal__dialog" data-ref="dialog" aria-label="{{ 'sections.quick_add.title' | t }}">
      <div class="modal__panel" data-ref="panel">
        <header class="modal__header">
          {%- comment -%}
            Visually hidden, and still here.

            The modal's visible heading is the product's own name, written by
            `sections/quick-add.liquid` into the body — which is what a customer
            needs to read, and which cannot be known until the fetch lands. This
            one stays because it is the heading a screen reader meets on entering
            the dialog, before the fetched content has been reached, and because
            removing it would leave the dialog's heading level empty.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <h2 class="modal__title visually-hidden">{{ 'sections.quick_add.title' | t }}</h2>
          {%- liquid
            assign quick_add_close_label = 'sections.quick_add.close' | t
          -%}
          {% render 'button',
             label: quick_add_close_label,
             id: 'QuickAddClose',
             class: 'close-quick-add',
             icon: 'close',
             variant: 'primary',
             size: 'small',
             icon_only: true,
             attributes: 'data-overlay-close' %}
        </header>

        {%- comment -%}
          `data-quick-add-content` and `data-product-root` live here rather than
          arriving with the fetched markup.

          They used to ride on the wrapper `sections/quick-add.liquid` renders,
          and reached this element only because `morph()` copied every attribute
          off that wrapper — which also overwrote `class="modal__body"` and took
          the body's padding and scrolling with it. `assets/dialog.js` now morphs
          the children only, so the two hooks have to be declared where the
          element actually is.

          They are what scope the modal's own `<variant-picker>`,
          `<product-form>` and `<quick-add-summary>` to it. Without them a modal
          opened over a product page finds the *page's* picker and form, and
          choosing a flavour in the modal changes the variant behind it.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div
          class="modal__body"
          data-ref="body"
          data-lenis-prevent
          data-quick-add-content
          data-product-root
        ></div>

        {%- comment -%}
          The loading state, and it is a sibling of the body rather than the
          body's first child.

          It used to be a "Loading product" paragraph inside it, which meant it
          existed exactly once: `applyHTML` replaces the body with the fetched
          section, so from the second product onwards there was no loading state
          at all — opening a different one showed the previous one's markup until
          the new one arrived. Out here it survives every fetch, because nothing
          ever writes over it.

          `quick-add-modal` carries `data-loading` through `setLoading()` for the
          length of the request, so the spinner needs no state of its own and
          cannot be left turning by a path that forgot to clear one.

          The words stay, hidden. `aria-busy` on the body says *that* something
          is happening; this is the only thing that says what, and a screen
          reader entering the dialog before the fetch lands would otherwise meet
          an empty region. It is `display: none` when idle, so it is out of the
          accessibility tree rather than being read out on a modal that has
          already loaded.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="quick-add__loader">
          {% render 'icon', icon: 'loader', size: 28 %}
          <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'sections.quick_add.loading' | t }}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </dialog>
  </quick-add-modal>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if settings.enable_image_zoom -%}
  <media-zoom-modal id="MediaZoom" class="media-zoom-modal modal">
    <dialog class="modal__dialog" data-ref="dialog" aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.open_zoom' | t }}">
      <div class="modal__panel" data-ref="panel">
        {%- liquid
          assign media_zoom_close_label = 'accessibility.close_zoom' | t
        -%}
        {% render 'button',
           label: media_zoom_close_label,
           id: 'MediaZoomClose',
           class: 'close-media-zoom',
           icon: 'close',
           variant: 'primary',
           size: 'small',
           icon_only: true,
           attributes: 'data-overlay-close' %}

        <div class="media-zoom-modal__viewport" data-ref="viewport" data-lenis-prevent>
          {%- comment -%}
            `width` and `height` are required on every `<img>` - without them the
            browser has no aspect ratio to reserve and the layout shifts when the
            file arrives.

            This one has no meaningful values to give: the source is written by
            `media-zoom.js` when a customer opens the modal, and it is a different
            photograph every time. The placeholder square satisfies the ratio, and
            `auto` on both axes hands the used size straight back to the image, so
            it still renders at its natural dimensions - which is what the zoom
            pans and scales.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <img
            data-ref="image"
            alt=""
            width="1000"
            height="1000"
            style="inline-size: auto; block-size: auto;"
          >
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          Where the zoom level is announced.

          It used to be written as `aria-label` on `<media-zoom-modal>` itself.
          A custom element with no role is `generic`, and `aria-label` is
          prohibited there - the audit is right, and the attribute was doing
          nothing either way: a label on a container is read when focus enters
          it, not when its value changes, so the level was never announced.

          A live region is read on change, which is what a zoom control needs.
          `role="status"` is polite by definition, so it waits for a pause rather
          than interrupting.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <span class="visually-hidden" role="status" data-ref="zoomStatus"></span>

        <div class="media-zoom-modal__controls">
          <button type="button" data-ref="zoomOut" aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.zoom_out' | t }}">
            {% render 'icon', icon: 'minus' %}
          </button>
          <button type="button" data-ref="reset" aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.reset_zoom' | t }}">
            {% render 'icon', icon: 'refresh' %}
          </button>
          <button type="button" data-ref="zoomIn" aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.zoom_in' | t }}">
            {% render 'icon', icon: 'plus' %}
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </dialog>
  </media-zoom-modal>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if settings.enable_compare -%}
  <product-compare
    id="ProductCompare"
    class="product-compare"
    data-section-id="compare"
    data-limit="{{ settings.compare_limit | default: 4 }}"
  >
    <button type="button" class="product-compare__bar" data-ref="open" hidden></button>
    <span class="visually-hidden" data-ref="count">0</span>

    <drawer-component
      id="CompareDrawer"
      class="compare-drawer drawer"
      data-placement="bottom"
    >
      <dialog class="drawer__dialog" data-ref="dialog" aria-label="{{ 'products.compare.drawer_title' | t }}">
        <div class="drawer__panel" data-ref="panel">
          <header class="drawer__header">
            <h2 class="drawer__title">{{ 'products.compare.drawer_title' | t }}</h2>
            {%- liquid
              assign compare_drawer_close_label = 'accessibility.close' | t
            -%}
            {% render 'button',
               label: compare_drawer_close_label,
               id: 'CompareDrawerClose',
               class: 'close-compare-drawer',
               icon: 'close',
               variant: 'primary',
               size: 'small',
               icon_only: true,
               attributes: 'data-overlay-close' %}
          </header>

          <div class="drawer__body" data-ref="body" data-lenis-prevent>
            <div data-ref="content" data-compare-content></div>
          </div>

          <footer class="drawer__footer">
            <button type="button" class="button button--secondary" data-ref="clear">
              {{ 'products.compare.clear_all' | t }}
            </button>
          </footer>
        </div>
      </dialog>
    </drawer-component>
  </product-compare>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:sections.overlays.name",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "t:sections.overlays.paragraph"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}